course: dictionary
lesson: 9011
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word skin is a good example. Read the words around it first, then choose the meaning that fits. In «I skinned my knee», skinned is a verb, so it means the skin was scraped off.
explain: In «a banana skin», skin means the outer layer of a fruit. In «a skin on hot milk», it means a thin layer that forms on top of a liquid. The words banana and milk are the context clues that tell you which meaning to use.
explain: Some words sit in phrases with their own meaning. «By the skin of your teeth» means only just. Ask yourself: does the meaning make sense if I take each word on its own? If it doesn't, the words may be a phrase.
fact: Skin is the body's biggest organ.
fact: Skin protects us from germs, keeps water in, and helps us feel touch, heat and pain.
fact: Sweat cools us down, so skin helps keep our body at a steady temperature.
fact: «Skin» can be a noun, the outer covering of a body or the outer layer of a fruit, or a verb, to scrape the skin off.
fact: «Ice on bare skin can burn», so wrap ice or frozen peas in a cloth first.
exercise: «Super glue sticks skin together in seconds, so only an adult uses it.» What does skin mean here? | *The outer covering of a body | A thin layer on top of milk | To scrape something off | The outer layer of a banana
exercise: «I skinned my knee on the playground.» What does skinned mean? | *Scraped the skin off | Washed it clean in the sink | Warmed it up near the fire | Peeled a banana
exercise: «There was a skin on the hot milk.» What does skin mean in this sentence? | The outer covering of a person | A kind of blanket | To scrape something off | *A thin layer that forms on top of a liquid
test: «Ice or frozen peas should be wrapped in a cloth, because ice on bare skin can burn.» Why should the ice be wrapped in a cloth? | To hide it from the sun | To keep it cold for longer | To make the peas taste better | *To stop it burning the skin
test: What does the phrase «by the skin of your teeth» mean? | Bright and shiny | Fast and loud | Very angry | *Only just
exam: What job does skin do for the body? | *Helps us feel touch, heat and pain | Pumps blood around the body | Makes food for the body | Makes the bones grow longer
